A||||6||RZURCTN||Front suspension problem......||||5||
Z||000000||RZURCTN||01-17-2000||11:27 PM||||Whenever I'm driving down the freeway the front end feels really loose.  The whole car kinda sways back and forth in the ruts.  I'm not a suspension guru, so I haven't a clue what's going on.  I checked it out underneath, but could find no problems evident to the naked eye.  Anyone have an idea for me?  It would be greatly appreciated!  The steering is fine around town and the only time I notice it is at higher speeds.  Thanks!||||reg||5
Z||000001||lowCG||01-18-2000||12:47 AM||||That's what any old,stock Fiero will feel like going down the road,it's caused by aero-lift from under the hood behind the radiator,probably sloppy balljoints,wheel bearings,worn or funky shocks/springs,bad alignment,tires that are worn,low on air pressure,or of poor quality.<BR>I'd start with tires/alignment/shocks first;if the suspension parts are worn,a good shop will let you know if the car can't be reliably aligned when this is done.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Phil||01-18-2000||06:27 AM||||Rear tire air pressure can have a great effect on handeling -easy to check and free||||reg||1
Z||000003||Carrolles||01-18-2000||08:14 AM||||Although I've never had this problem with my Fiero I did have it on another car.  It turned out to be tires.  New tires fixed it.<P>When I replaced the struts on my Fiero I did not bother trying to align the rear suspension since I was getting new tires the next day.  On the drive to the shop for alignment and tires I had a hard time keeping the back of the car from passing the front.  Rear alignment is critical to handling.  What was happening was that since the rear wheels were not aligned (I'm not sure of the proper terminology but the wheels were tilted out at the top) as I would cross the crown in the road or turn slightly the load would shift to one tire which would try to track to the inside.  This would set up an ossilation like you see when a trailer starts to sway back and forth.  Very annoying at 35mph.  I'm just glad I did not take the interstaet to the shop.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Spyder||01-18-2000||11:33 AM||||Here is a neat story:  I went to buy an 88 Fiero. The old guy at the used car place said it had a bad and dangerous suspension.  So he sold it to me cheap, a steal, so I drove it the  5 miles to my home. I barely got home, the rear wheel was bouncing, the front swaying back and forth, Very loose, Very Scary!! <BR>I checked out the suspension,  The Lug Nuts Were Loose, that’s it. After that it drove like a champ.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||Deer_Slayer||01-18-2000||03:06 PM||||You could also try replacing the stabilizer bar bushings in the front.  Before replacing them on my 86, the front shook and steering was rough.  Afterwards, it was like driving a different car.  The kits are fairly cheep and the bushings are easy to replace. ||||reg||1
Z||000006||Fieroguy1||01-18-2000||04:23 PM||||I,have the same problem on one of my Fieros.<BR>Check the tie rods especially the inner ones.<BR>Which is the culprit on mine.||||reg||1
